      "id": 1,
      "text": "Colfax earned its name when pioneer Jared Berarducci renamed the settlement after Vice President Schuyler Colfax in 1873.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colfax%2C_Washington",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Nestled where the north and south forks of the Palouse River meet, Colfax is surrounded by wheat‑covered hills.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colfax%2C_Washington",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first Columbia and Palouse Railroad train rolled into Colfax on November 10, 1883, opening the town to the nation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historylink.org/File/9580",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1906, the Colfax Electric Company completed a 60,000‑volt line, electrifying the town for the first time.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historylink.org/File/9580",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Every July, Colfax celebrates Concrete River Days, a festival that highlights the town’s concrete‑lined riverbed built for flood control.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
